1. An odor-sensitive building is in any case a building or part of a building with:
a. residential function and secondary use functions thereof; b. educational function and secondary functions thereof;
c. healthcare function with bed area and secondary use functions thereof;
d. meeting function for childcare with bed area and ancillary functions thereof.
2. The first paragraph does not apply to a part of a building as referred to in that paragraph if the environmental plan in that part does not allow: a. residential function;
b. educational function;
c. healthcare function with bed area;
d. childcare meeting function with bed area;
e. meeting function that is an additional use function of a residential function; or
f. meeting function that is an additional use function of a healthcare function with bed area.
3. An odor-sensitive building also includes a building that is not yet present, but may be constructed for a deviating activity on the basis of an environmental plan or environmental permit.
4. The environmental plan may designate other odor-sensitive buildings or parts of buildings, provided that they are mainly occupied by people.
5. In the environmental plan, paragraph 5.1.4.6 can be applied accordingly to locations where people mainly reside.

